  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Frame",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Quiet Frame\"**\n\nfiles.gg is a dark, calm frame, and the file is the picture. In the viewer, the image, video, PDF or code fills a card whose size the media decides, with a slim footer underneath: the name, the size, when it expires, and a few actions. The dashboard carries the same calm into a tool you manage files with: a sidebar, page titles, and lists of rows on tonal layers. Nothing in the interface competes with what was shared.\n\nGreen carries the meaning. It comes from the logo's folded tile and marks the action to take, the place you are in, links, and focus. Everything else is greys separated by tone and a single hairline. Depth comes from stepping between those greys, not from shadows. Only floating things (the viewer card and dialogs) cast one.\n\nThe form language is soft and friendly, echoing the rounded tile in the logo: generous 12 px corners on controls, 16 px on cards, full pills on filter chips, and 42 px controls that are easy to hit on a phone.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark only for now. The raw tokens sit on `:root` in `web/src/styles/theme.css` so a light theme can redefine them in one place.",
      "One accent, Signal Green, used for meaning and never as decoration on large areas.",
      "Flat tonal layers, a 1 px hairline, and one card shadow for things that float.",
      "The system font stack; no webfonts.",
      "Soft, rounded controls and cards that echo the logo tile."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Green Means Go Rule",
        "body": "Signal Green marks what you can act on, where you are, and the brand. It never fills a surface larger than a button. If a screen has green that does neither, remove it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Borrowed Greys Rule",
        "body": "The neutrals (`bg`, `surface`, `surface-2`, `line`, `text`, `muted`) currently match Discord's dark theme exactly. That match is not part of the identity and is slated to change. When it does, edit the values on `:root` in `theme.css` only, keep the same tonal order (well < bg < surface < surface-2 < line), and keep WCAG 2.2 AA contrast for `muted` on `surface-2`.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The System Voice Rule",
        "body": "No webfonts. The viewer must boot from inlined data with a small bundle, and the wordmark is drawn from strokes, so it needs no font. Hierarchy comes from size and weight (400, 500, 600), never from a second family.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Numbers Line Up Rule",
        "body": "Sizes, counts and storage figures use tabular numerals (`tabular-nums`) wherever they sit in columns or update while you watch.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Media Decides Rule",
        "body": "In the viewer, never give the card a fixed width or a layout grid. The file sets the size, and the interface fits around it.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Shadow Rule",
        "body": "In-flow surfaces are flat. Only something floating above the page (the viewer card, a dialog, a menu) casts the card shadow, and nothing gets a second, different shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tile Echo Rule",
        "body": "A corner is a control (12 px), a card (16 px) or a pill. Don't invent sizes in between.",
        "section": "shapes"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Reds Rule",
        "body": "Red text and red fills are different tokens. Use `danger` for text, borders and tints, and `danger-fill` behind white text. One red can't pass AA both ways on these dark surfaces.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Quiet Viewer Rule",
        "body": "On the viewer and album pages, no button has a green fill. Copy link is a grey square with a green icon; the file is the brightest thing on the page.",
        "section": "colors"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** let the media set the viewer card's size, and reserve its space from the width and height before it loads.",
      "**Do** keep at most one primary (Signal Green) button per card or toolbar in the dashboard; on the viewer, none (the Quiet Viewer Rule).",
      "**Do** separate layers by tone (Media Well < Frame Charcoal < Card Slate < Raised Slate) and a 1 px Hairline Grey, not by shadow.",
      "**Do** use 12 px corners on controls, 16 px on cards and dialogs, and full pills on chips, with controls 42 px tall.",
      "**Do** show status as a 10–15% tint of Alert Red or Caution Amber with a full border in the same colour at 40%.",
      "**Do** put white text only on `danger-fill`, never on `danger`.",
      "**Do** keep the 2 px Signal Green focus outline, offset 2 px, on every interactive element.",
      "**Do** meet WCAG 2.2 AA contrast for all text, including Quiet Grey on Raised Slate."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** drop the fold from the logo tile, draw the f in white, or use the lockup below about 120 px wide (see `branding/README.md`).",
      "**Don't** put a coloured stripe down one side of a rounded card (as `app/notices/NoticeCard.tsx` does today). Use a full border, a tint, or a leading icon.",
      "**Don't** fill a surface larger than a button with Signal Green, or use it for decoration.",
      "**Don't** add a webfont or a second type family.",
      "**Don't** add new shadow styles; floating things use the card shadow, everything else is flat.",
      "**Don't** treat today's Discord-matching greys as fixed identity; change them only on `:root` in `theme.css`."
    ]
  }
